0. Definitions.

"This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU Affero General Public License.

"Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds
of works, such as semiconductor masks.

"The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License. Each licensee is addressed as "you". "Licensees" and
"recipients" refer to individuals or organizations that receive the
Program.

"Modify" means to copy from or adapt all or part of the work in a
fashion requiring copyright permission, other than the making of an
exact copy. The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the
earlier work or a work "based on" the earlier work.

A "covered work" means either the unmodified Program or a work based
on the Program.

"Propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without
permission, would make you directly or indirectly liable for
infringement under applicable copyright law, except executing it on
a computer or modifying it in the privacy of your own home. "Convey"
a work means any kind of propagation that enables other parties to
make or receive copies. Mere interaction with a user through a
computer network, with no transfer of a copy, is not conveying.

An interactive user interface displays "Appropriate Legal Notices"
to the extent that it includes a convenient and prominently visible
feature that (1) displays an appropriate copyright notice, and (2)
tells the user that there is no warranty for the work (except to the
extent that warranties are implied), and that the user has the right
to copy and distribute the work, and that the user may modify the
work, and that there is a mechanism for doing so.

1. Source Code.

The "source code" for a work means the preferred form of the work
for making modifications to it. "Object code" means any non-source
form of a work.

A "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard or is so widely used that it can be considered standard for
products of the same type.

"System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, other
than the work as a whole, that (a) is included in the normal form of
packaging a Given Work, and (b) is not intended to be modified or
repackaged by the user. An example of a System Library is the
standard library included with an operating system. A "Subsystem" of
an executable work is a part of it that is specifically designed to
interact with a particular kind of hardware or software, and is not
a System Library.

"Corresponding Source" for a work in object code form means all the
source code needed to generate, install, and run the object code and
to operate it in the same way. This includes the source code for all
subsystems and system libraries except System Libraries. The
Corresponding Source does not need to include source code for System
Libraries that are implicitly available in the execution environment
of the work.

